    <item>
      <title>Daily Podcast 2026-07-17</title>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">https://github.com/vllorens/microbiome_digest/releases/download/episode-2026-07-17/podcast_2026-07-17.mp3</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 17 Jul 2026 08:00:00 GMT</pubDate>
      <enclosure url="https://github.com/vllorens/microbiome_digest/releases/download/episode-2026-07-17/podcast_2026-07-17.mp3" length="28736972" type="audio/mpeg" />
      <description>Dietary patterns directly shape gut microbiota composition and function, which in turn drives the onset and severity of irritable bowel syndrome and inflammatory bowel disease, according to a review in PubMed’s Reviews on environmental health. | Gut microbes enable the woodboring beetle Streltzoviella insularis to break down the defensive secondary metabolites of green ash (Fraxinus pennsylvanica), boosting its ability to feed on this widespread urban tree. | The meta‑analysis published in BMC Gastroenterology set out to clarify two linked questions: how gut microbiota dysbiosis relates to the development of ulcerative colitis, and whether fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) can effectively treat the condition.</description>
      <itunes:author>Veronica Llorens</itunes:author>
      <itunes:summary>Dietary patterns directly shape gut microbiota composition and function, which in turn drives the onset and severity of irritable bowel syndrome and inflammatory bowel disease, according to a review in PubMed’s Reviews on environmental health. | Gut microbes enable the woodboring beetle Streltzoviella insularis to break down the defensive secondary metabolites of green ash (Fraxinus pennsylvanica), boosting its ability to feed on this widespread urban tree. | The meta‑analysis published in BMC Gastroenterology set out to clarify two linked questions: how gut microbiota dysbiosis relates to the development of ulcerative colitis, and whether fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) can effectively treat the condition.</itunes:summary>
      <itunes:explicit>false</itunes:explicit>
      <itunes:image href="https://vllorens.github.io/microbiome_digest/cover.png" />
    </item>
